 ## Background
-The method was developed by Livat, Shen, and Johnson as part of the NOISE (Near Optimal Isosurface Extraction) algorithm [S1, book]. It addresses the limitations of previous interval search methods that ordered intervals by only one dimension (min or max), requiring complex merging of lists [S1, book]. By using span space and kd-trees, it simultaneously orders cells by both minimum and maximum values [S1, book]. The worst-case search complexity analysis was developed by Lee and Wong [S1, book].
+The method was developed by Livnat, Shen, and Johnson as part of the NOISE (Near Optimal Isosurface Extraction) algorithm [S3, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of previous interval search methods that ordered intervals by only one dimension (min or max), requiring complex merging of lists [S2, book]. By using span space and kd-trees, it simultaneously orders cells by both minimum and maximum values [S2, book]. The worst-case search complexity analysis was developed by Lee and Wong [S2, book].
 
